1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,966
Your backtest and your live system are usually two different codebases.

2
00:00:04,016 --> 00:00:05,550
That's why they drift.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:07,215
You describe it once, in Rust.

4
00:00:07,265 --> 00:00:12,140
For example: an order book, fanned out to each side, recombined into a quote.

5
00:00:12,333 --> 00:00:13,712
Under the hood it's a graph.

6
00:00:13,762 --> 00:00:17,217
Each node runs once per cycle, even where branches recombine.

7
00:00:17,267 --> 00:00:18,293
Run it historically.

8
00:00:18,343 --> 00:00:20,850
It's an instant, deterministic replay.

9
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:22,260
Run the same graph live.

10
00:00:22,310 --> 00:00:23,669
Paced by the wall clock.

11
00:00:23,719 --> 00:00:25,079
Same values, same order.

12
00:00:25,129 --> 00:00:26,417
Only the clock changes.

13
00:00:26,467 --> 00:00:27,282
One definition.

14
00:00:27,332 --> 00:00:28,090
Two run modes.

15
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:30,250
The implementations can't drift.

16
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:30,836
Wingfoil.

17
00:00:30,886 --> 00:00:33,130
Stream processing in Rust.
